Not "would", just "might".  Well, it depens a lot on what you consider 
"valid".

One point of my argument was simply that if the basis of observations is 
too small, due to the different conditions in different cases, you 
cannot conclude much.  I she had made twelve observations in the same 
environment, she would have had a better basis for expecting the same 
result in a new experiment, *again* under the same conditions.  If she 
gets the same result under two different conditions, she would in 
principle need more experiments (roughly 24) to get the same kind of 
observational basis for reasoning about both conditions.

What you suggest is generalization.  This is a legitimate strategy of 
reasoning, but of course less justified than the simpler assumption 
"given the same conditions and the same behaviour on my side, the same 
will happen".
